\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{An even integral rotation}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{In the integral plane of \ref{fdip-00YF}, put
#{R=e_1e_2\in\operatorname{Cl}(q)}. The Clifford relations are
#{e_1^2=e_2^2=1} and #{e_1e_2=-e_2e_1}, so
#{R^2=-1}, #{R^{-1}=-R}, and #{\alpha(R)=R}.
Multiplication gives #{\rho(R)e_i=Re_iR^{-1}=-e_i} for both
basis vectors. Thus #{R\in\Gamma} and #{\rho(R)=-\bbI_V}
preserves #{b}. The even subgroup #{H=\langle R\rangle} has
order #{4}. To verify that its 4 powers are distinct, use the
Clifford action #{e_i\mapsto(e_i\wedge)+(e_i\cdot)} of
\ref{fdip-0092}. Applied to #{1\in E}, the powers
#{1,R,R^2,R^3} give
##{1,\quad e_1\wedge e_2,\quad -1,\quad -e_1\wedge e_2.}
These are distinct over #{\mathbb Z} and #{\mathbb Q}, so the
4 Clifford elements are distinct.}

\p{In the ordered internal exterior basis of \ref{fdip-00YF},
##{A(R)=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,1).}
The top exterior vector receives two minus signs. This action
preserves the integral Gram matrix #{\bbI_4}; the scalar element
#{R^2=-1} acts as the identity on the whole exterior module.}
